<details>
<summary>Click to see the solution</summary>

**Key Concept:** Just like compile-time factorial, compile-time Fibonacci requires explicit specializations as base cases to stop the template recursion. Without them, the compiler would recurse infinitely.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Primary template: Fib(N) = Fib(N-1) + Fib(N-2)
template <unsigned N>
unsigned long Fib() {
    return Fib<N - 1>() + Fib<N - 2>();
}

// Base case: Fib(1) = 1
template <>
unsigned long Fib<1>() {
    return 1;
}

// Base case: Fib(2) = 1
template <>
unsigned long Fib<2>() {
    return 1;
}

int main() {
    cout << Fib<1>() << endl;   // 1
    cout << Fib<2>() << endl;   // 1
    cout << Fib<3>() << endl;   // 2
    cout << Fib<5>() << endl;   // 5
    cout << Fib<10>() << endl;  // 55
